<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/glossary#prakrit">Glossary</a> · Term</p><h1 class="enc-title">Prakrit</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Prakrits</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Prakrit is the name for the vernacular Middle Indo-Aryan languages of ancient <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>, related to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sanskrit">Sanskrit</a> but simpler in sound and grammar. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ashoka">Ashoka</a>'s edicts and many early royal inscriptions are in Prakrit, and Pali and the languages of Jain scripture are forms of it. Courts used it in inscriptions and coins until Sanskrit largely replaced it from the 4th century CE.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/prakrit.jpg" alt="Prakrit" width="320" height="231" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: 6th century CE engraver. inked impression and photograph by J.F. Fleet (1847 – 21 February 1917) in 1888 · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Word_for_Prakrit_Praakritee_in_the_Mandsaur_stone_inscription_of_Yashodharman-Vishnuvardhana_532_CE.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The court of Kannauj supported the poet and critic Rajashekhara, author of the <b>Prakrit</b> play Karpuramanjari and the treatise on poetics Kavyamimamsa.</blockquote><a href="/empire/gurjara-pratihara">Gurjara-Pratihara · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>From Apollodotus I onward almost all coins were bilingual, Greek on the obverse and <b>Prakrit</b> in the Kharoshthi script on the reverse, and from Apollodotus II, about 80 BCE, Kharoshthi letters served as mintmarks beside Greek monograms, which suggests local technicians in the mints.</blockquote><a href="/empire/indo-greek-kingdom">Indo-Greek Kingdom · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>The Pallavas first appear in the late third and fourth centuries CE in copper-plate charters written in <b>Prakrit</b>, such as the Mayidavolu and Hirahadagalli grants of Shivaskandavarman, issued from Kanchipuram and recording gifts of land in the Andhra country and the Bellary region.</blockquote><a href="/empire/pallava-dynasty">Pallava dynasty · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Satavahana kings were known by their mothers' names, such as Gautamiputra, 'son of Gautami', and some of their silver coins bear legends in both <b>Prakrit</b> and an early Dravidian language.</blockquote><a href="/empire/satavahana-dynasty">Satavahana dynasty · At a glance</a></li><li><blockquote>…in Gujarat, in 453 or 466 CE, where it was fixed in the form known today: the Agamas, about forty-five texts in Ardhamagadhi <b>Prakrit</b>, among them the Acaranga Sutra, which describes Mahavira’s ascetic life, and the Kalpa Sutra, recited during Paryushana, which tells the lives of the Tirthankaras.</blockquote><a href="/religion/jainism">Jainism · Texts &amp; Teachers</a></li><li><blockquote>Pali is a Middle Indo-Aryan language, and nearly every word in it has cognates in the other Middle Indo-Aryan languages, the <b>Prakrits</b>.</blockquote><a href="/language/pali">Pali · Origins</a></li></ul></section>
